<h2 id="needs">What FFmpeg Needs</h2>

<p class="with-icon">
  <span class="pull-left"><i class="fa fa-wrench fa-5x"></i></span>
  FFmpeg is used by nearly every product that touches audio or video,
  but the work that keeps it secure and reliable is largely done by
  volunteers.
  Support in the following areas has the largest effect:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>
    <b>Maintainers:</b> people who review changes, fix bugs and keep
    their part of FFmpeg working. Companies that depend on FFmpeg can
    help most by letting their developers maintain the code they use.
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Hardware:</b> access to any hardware you want FFmpeg to keep
    working reliably on, preferably as a VM or an account on a machine
    you maintain. It lets the project test every change there (CI and
    <a href="http://fate.ffmpeg.org">FATE</a>) and run
    <a href="https://code.ffmpeg.org/michaelni/Fairies">Fairy</a>, the
    project's assistant that reviews pull requests and investigates
    bug reports.
  </li>
  <li>
    <b>Money:</b> donations in USD or EUR through
    <a href="donations.html">SPI or GitHub Sponsors</a> pay for servers,
    hardware and other project expenses.
  </li>
</ul>

<br>

